// =============================================================================
// Event Modeling schema.
//
// PURPOSE -- read this first.
//
// This file defines the data schema for Event Modeling -- events, commands,
// read models, processors, UIs, personas, swim lanes, slices, storyboards.
// These are design-time descriptions of how a problem behaves, intended to
// be authored and manipulated by humans and by MCP tools. They are not
// runtime payloads, not service contracts, not packages.
//
// The gRPC service that exposes this schema as a queryable, denormalized
// keyspace (EventModelService) lives in trogonatlas.api.eventmodel.v1alpha1,
// layered on top of this package via trogonatlas.catalog.v1alpha1 (dependency
// direction: api -> catalog -> eventmodel -> {annotation, type}). An MCP tool is
// implemented as a thin adapter over that gRPC service: MCP tools call
// gRPC; the service is the source of truth; this schema is the contract
// between them. There is no second data model.
//
// What is explicitly OUT of scope (do not add to this file):
//    - Package / module / workspace containers.
//    - Manifests, declared dependencies, or version-resolution rules.
//    - IAM, ownership, permissions, or tenant boundaries.
//    - Code-generation, deployment, or wire-protocol concerns of the
//      modeled domains themselves (this file's job is to describe the model,
//      not to ship the systems it models).
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// DESIGN DECISIONS (rationale for each non-obvious choice).
//
// 1.  Problem-modeling, not software-shipping. Everything cosmetic to
//      distribution lives outside this schema. MCP tools handle storage,
//      discovery, IAM, and codegen.
//
// 2.  Identity = (namespace, slug, version). namespace is a free-form
//      disambiguator chosen by the model's owner; it is NOT a package name,
//      dependency target, or resolved path. slug is the human name
//      ("order.placed"). version is a monotonic uint64 breaking-change
//      counter (semver-major-only at this layer); doc/scenario tweaks do
//      NOT bump it. Refs always carry an exact triple; there is no implicit
//      "latest" in the data itself -- `latest` is a service-level concept
//      (GetLatestVersion).
//
// 3.  Multi-version coexistence. Because version is part of identity, every
//      version of an entity is a distinct row in the keyspace. A slice can
//      reference Event v1 and another slice can reference Event v2 in the
//      same model; both resolve independently. This is the safety property
//      for linking: refactors never silently retarget existing references.
//
// 4.  Version lineage via `Id supersedes`. Every top-level entity declares
//      which prior version it replaces (if any) as a structural field, not
//      metadata. This lets tools walk the supersession chain ("what did v3
//      replace?") without scanning everything. Forward navigation
//      ("what replaced v1?") is a reverse-index query on the service.
//
// 5.  Typed Refs (EventRef, CommandRef, …) instead of a single generic
//      pointer. Prevents the wire from mixing kinds and keeps the graph
//      statically checkable without an enum tag in every pointer.
//
// 6.  Typed Edges carry metadata. Where a connection can hold design data
//      (slice endpoints, storyboard entry/outcome), the schema uses Edge
//      messages (Ref + doc + repeated Any). Distinct from Refs, which are
//      pure pointers used inside FieldSpec/Example/TypeRef.
//
// 7.  Universal metadata extension point. Every top-level entity carries
//      `repeated google.protobuf.Any metadata`. Notes, links, tags,
//      lifecycle status (draft/accepted/deprecated), invariants, tool
//      layout -- all attach here as typed annotations. New annotation kinds
//      do not require schema changes. FieldSpec and scenarios also carry
//      `metadata` for their own per-field and per-scenario annotations
//      (e.g. NoteAnnotation, OpenQuestionAnnotation). Traces do not carry
//      metadata; annotate the parent entity.
//
// 8.  Persona, not Actor. A Persona is a named human profile ("Bob the
//      manager"), always human. Systems are Processors; external systems
//      are ReadModels (with ExternalSource). Classic EM has no formal
//      persona; we add it because the same human profile is referenced
//      across many slices and needs a stable identifier.
//
// 9.  Swimlane = event-sourcing stream (NOT a visual band). It is the
//      consistency / transaction boundary for the events and commands on
//      it. Read models, UIs, processors, and personas are cross-cutting
//      and do not reference a swimlane.
//
// 10. Storyboard entry = ReadModel + Observer. Outcome = Events (UI
//      optional). Universal trigger shape: human-via-UI observes a
//      ReadModel, or automation observes a ReadModel. External webhooks
//      and schedules are ReadModels (ExternalSource / Clock-style). No
//      second category of trigger.
//
// 11. Four slice kinds only: CommandSlice, ReadModelSlice, UiSlice,
//      AutomationSlice. No Translation slice (translation = a ReadModel
//      with ExternalSource consumed by an AutomationSlice).
//      AutomationSlice reads ReadModels only (never raw events) and emits
//      at most ONE command per execution. Multi-step orchestration =
//      multiple AutomationSlices chained through the graph.
//      One slice is one moment, which is why displaying a read model is a
//      UiSlice rather than a field on the projection: a screen consumes a
//      read model exactly as a processor does, so the two consumers are
//      the same shape (ReadModel(s) -> UI, ReadModel(s) -> Processor) and
//      the projection (Event(s) -> ReadModel) owns neither.
//
// 12. Six allowed edges. Command->Event, Event->ReadModel, ReadModel->UI,
//      ReadModel->Processor, UI->Command, Processor->Command. Anything
//      else is structurally impossible by construction.
//
// 13. Scenarios use google.protobuf.Any for payloads (end-to-end proto:
//      schema and example values share one type system). The Any.type_url
//      identifies the generated proto for the referenced Event/Command/
//      ReadModel (codegen target derived from the type's FieldSpec).
//
// 14. StoryboardTraces are the data-completeness mechanism (consistent
//      values flowing through every step). No TraceKind enum -- trace
//      intent (happy / edge / failure / forbidden) lives in title + doc,
//      not in a categorization that's always arbitrary at the boundaries.
//      If a strong-typed "forbidden" assertion is ever needed, add a
//      separate ForbiddenAssertion message; do not re-introduce the enum.
//
// 15. Flat keyspace via `Entity`. Every addressable thing is wrappable in
//      the `Entity` oneof and uniquely identified by (EntityKind, Id).
//      This is what makes the service look like a denormalized map: one
//      bulk-get returns everything reachable, keyed by canonical Id string.
//
// 16. The gRPC service is the only contract. There is no separate REST
//      surface, no separate persistence schema, no separate MCP schema.
//      The MCP tool is a thin adapter: each MCP tool call maps 1:1 to one
//      RPC. This keeps a single source of truth and avoids drift.
//
// 17. Inference, not declaration. The schema does NOT have structural
//      FieldMapping / data-flow / completeness fields on slices. Adding
//      them would push humans into specifying, field by field, where every
//      value comes from -- which is coding in protobuf and defeats the
//      purpose of a description language. Instead, data flow and
//      information completeness are derived by the service from FieldSpec
//      + doc + graph structure, on demand (InferDataFlow,
//      CheckInformationCompleteness). The AI does the heavy lifting;
//      humans describe stickies and review the gaps the AI surfaces.
//      If a tool wants to cache AI conclusions, it parks them as `Any`
//      annotations in the existing `metadata` list -- never as new
//      structural fields. Re-running the analysis always works because
//      the model is self-describing; the cache is a convenience, not a
//      contract.
//
// 18. Flat keyspace is canonical; EventModel is a curated listing.
//      Every entity (Event, Command, ReadModel, Processor, UI, Persona,
//      Swimlane, Slice variants, Storyboard, EventModel) lives in the
//      flat (EntityKind, Id) keyspace and is fetched independently via
//      GetEntity. An EventModel does NOT inline its members; it holds
//      `repeated EntityRef members` -- a curated list of the entities
//      that conceptually belong together. This removes the two-source-
//      of-truth risk where an entity inside `EventModel.types` could
//      drift from the same entity addressed via `GetEntity`.
//      Projection RPCs (GetSliceProjection, GetStoryboardProjection,
//      GetEventModelProjection) hydrate the refs into a self-contained
//      subgraph for callers that want everything in one response.
//
// 19. Polling, not streaming, for change notifications. MCP tools issue
//      unary calls; long-lived streams are awkward over the MCP transport
//      and complicate tool semantics ("did the stream end? did I miss
//      events?"). The service exposes ListChanges with an opaque cursor
//      instead: callers poll, the server records change events, the
//      cursor advances. Streaming can be added later if a non-MCP
//      consumer needs it; it would not replace the polling RPC.
//
// 20. Canonical annotation baseline. The schema defines a small set of
//      standard annotation messages (NoteAnnotation, LinkAnnotation,
//      TagAnnotation, LifecycleAnnotation, OpenQuestionAnnotation,
//      Uuidv5IdentityAnnotation, CodeRefAnnotation) that
//      any tool can pack into `metadata`. This is the minimum interop
//      surface so two tools sharing a model can agree on at least notes,
//      links, tags, lifecycle status, open questions, and code linkage.
//      Tools may define additional annotation types beyond this baseline.
//
// 21. Optimistic concurrency via opaque etag. Id.version is the
//      breaking-change counter, not a per-write revision (Decision #2).
//      Within a single (namespace, slug, version) row, non-breaking edits
//      (doc tweaks, scenario additions, metadata changes) can still race
//      between concurrent MCP clients. The service therefore exposes an
//      opaque `etag` on reads and accepts it as `if_match` on PutEntity
//      and DeleteEntity. ABORTED is returned on mismatch. The etag is
//      opaque -- tools must not parse or compare it structurally. This is
//      the standard gRPC pattern (matches google.api.ResourceInfo) and
//      keeps concurrency control out of Id semantics.
//
// 22. Capabilities and limits are discoverable, not assumed. MCP adapters
//      bootstrap against an unknown server implementation. A single
//      GetServerInfo RPC reports which optional RPCs the server actually
//      implements (search, change feed, AI analysis, diff, impact,
//      extract-subgraph) and what numeric limits apply (max page size,
//      max impact depth, max projection size). MCP tools that need any
//      of these features should probe first rather than catching
//      UNIMPLEMENTED errors. ListNamespaces complements this for
//      discovery: tools can enumerate what models exist before issuing
//      namespace-filtered queries.
//
// 23. Multi-entity atomicity via BatchMutate. A real edit usually touches
//      several entities at once (renaming a field on an Event and
//      updating every Slice that references it; splitting one Slice into
//      two). Single-entity PutEntity / DeleteEntity cannot express this
//      without leaving the model temporarily inconsistent. BatchMutate
//      takes an ordered list of Put/Delete ops and applies them
//      all-or-nothing. Per-op if_match is honored. The RPC itself always
//      returns OK; success or failure is carried in the response's
//      `status` field along with the index and diagnostics of the failing
//      op. This keeps MCP tools from having to parse gRPC status codes to
//      distinguish "the RPC failed" from "one op in the batch failed."
//      Validate-only mode previews the full batch without persisting,
//      giving MCP tools a safe way to ask "what would this change set
//      do?" before committing.
//
// 24. TIME ONLY POINTS FORWARD. The model is a timeline: every edge a tool
//      draws or derives points from an earlier moment to a later one --
//      never backward. Recurrence of the same TYPE (a screen shown again, a
//      view updated again, a lifecycle re-entered) is a NEW occurrence, a
//      new moment of the same entity; it is never an arrow into the past,
//      because a backward arrow destroys the ability to read time off the
//      graph. Concretely: read models and UIs repeat per slice (one sticky
//      per moment), automations observe the LATEST prior occurrence of
//      their view, projections land adjacent to their emitters (validated:
//      PROJECTION_BEFORE_EMITTER / PROJECTION_NOT_ADJACENT), scenario
//      given/emit histories must walk a lane's transitions forward
//      (validated: SCENARIO_TRANSITION_VIOLATION), and lifecycle cycles
//      (Swimlane.transitions back to an earlier state) render as forward
//      re-entry stubs, never as backward edges.
//
//      This binds every DERIVED graph too, at every aggregation level --
//      sitemaps, summaries, roll-ups. Collapsing moments into type-level
//      nodes is where backward edges sneak in: two types that "point at
//      each other" claim to be each other's past, and time becomes
//      unreadable. The rule: collapse by type only as far as the graph
//      stays forward; the first arrow that would point backward instead
//      mints a NEW occurrence of its target (Sign-up #1 -> Settings #1 ->
//      Sign-up #2). Multiple occurrences of one type are grouping, not
//      duplication.
//
// 25. PROJECT MANAGEMENT LAYERS ON TOP, NEVER INSIDE. Delivery status
//      (todo, in progress, blocked, done) is an opinion ABOUT the model,
//      not a property OF it: the team's process decides it, not the slice.
//      Status therefore lives in Tracker entities -- denormalized overlays
//      holding EntityRef pointers plus TrackStatus -- and design entities
//      carry no status fields. This keeps status flips from mutating
//      design history, lets any entity kind be tracked, and lets several
//      trackers (per team, per milestone) overlay one model independently.
//
// 27. A NAMESPACE IS A BOUNDED CONTEXT, AND BoundedContext IS ITS ROOT
//      DOCUMENT. The namespace is the boundary where the rules bite (slug
//      uniqueness, free internal refs, the cross-context seam invariant).
//      Its charter -- title, doc, links, metadata -- lives on a dedicated
//      BoundedContext entity whose slug EQUALS the namespace (validated),
//      which makes it naturally unique. Event models remain free-form
//      curations WITHIN a context -- a feature, a flow, a release -- and a
//      namespace may hold many of them. The Id field stays `namespace`: it
//      names the MECHANISM (the scope where slugs are unique and the
//      coupling rules bite), while declaring a BoundedContext is the
//      ASSERTION that the scope is a context. The layers may diverge -- a
//      shared `vendors` namespace holding ExternalSystems for several
//      consumers is a naming scope that is not a bounded context.
//
// 28. THE SCHEMA MODELS DECISIONS AND DATA, NEVER IMPLEMENTATION. The
//      subject matter is what survives a full rewrite of the codebase:
//      STRATEGIC design (which bounded contexts exist, their charters, how
//      they relate through seams, where investment goes) and the BEHAVIORAL
//      CONTRACT (which facts happen, in what order, carrying which fields --
//      slices, storyboards, lifecycles, field types). Both layers are data
//      about the business, and the behavioral layer stays in scope because
//      it is data about behavior, not technology. The hard floor: nothing
//      about classes, services, repositories, frameworks, persistence, or
//      code structure -- tactical DDD patterns live in the code they
//      organize, and modeling them here would only go stale. The closest
//      the schema gets is a pointer out (Component.tech, UI.design_ref),
//      never the design itself. When in doubt ask: would this survive
//      reimplementing the system in another language? If not, it does not
//      belong here.
//
// 29. THE PROBLEM SPACE IS A KNOWLEDGE GRAPH: Domain and Subdomain are
//      first-class entities, pure facts about the business with no behavior,
//      no slices, and no time. Containment is exact and small: a Domain
//      contains Subdomains (a partition of the problem); a namespace contains
//      entities (mechanical, slug-scoped); a BoundedContext IS the assertion
//      about its namespace. A Subdomain NEVER contains bounded contexts --
//      the spaces meet only through the `realizes` MAPPING on
//      BoundedContext, pointing solution at problem. Classification
//      (core/supporting/generic) lives on the SUBDOMAIN, because it is a
//      property of the problem -- a context inherits "where investment goes"
//      through realizes, so reclassifying a subdomain reprices every context
//      realizing it. Cardinality is honest, not idealized: 1:1 is the goal,
//      one subdomain realized by several contexts is deliberate splitting,
//      one context realizing several subdomains is a monolith confessing.
//      Domain/Subdomain live in a namespace named after the domain -- a
//      naming scope that is deliberately NOT a bounded context (legal per
//      Decision #27). `realizes` is the SECOND legal cross-namespace ref
//      class after the event seam: strategic mapping is knowledge, not
//      behavioral coupling -- no data flows through it. The three lenses:
//      namespace = mechanics (where rules bite), bounded context = semantics
//      (what words mean), subdomain = economics (what it is worth).
//
// 30. SEAMS CARRY DATA; RELATIONSHIPS CARRY INTENT. The derived context map
//      proves THAT one context feeds another (event -> subscription view);
//      it cannot prove the AGREEMENT behind it -- who moves when the
//      upstream event wants a breaking change. That intent is declared on
//      the DOWNSTREAM context (the subscriber knows its own posture):
//      CUSTOMER_SUPPLIER (downstream has a voice in upstream planning),
//      CONFORMIST (downstream takes what it gets), PARTNERSHIP (the two
//      succeed or fail together), SEPARATE_WAYS (deliberate
//      non-integration -- declared so silence reads as a decision, not an
//      omission). Intent and data must agree: declaring a relationship no
//      seam supports warns (RELATIONSHIP_WITHOUT_SEAM), declaring
//      SEPARATE_WAYS while a seam exists is an error
//      (RELATIONSHIP_CONTRADICTS_SEAM), and a seam with no declared intent
//      gets an Info nudge (SEAM_WITHOUT_RELATIONSHIP).
//
// 31. DISCONNECTS ARE DATA WHEN DECLARED. An entity that is a member of
//      an EventModel but is not produced by, consumed by, or fed into any
//      slice in any storyboard of that model is DISCONNECTED. Disconnects
//      are not always errors -- a subscription view may wait for its
//      consumer, an audit log may have no UI yet, a niche flow (cancel,
//      dispute) may be deferred to a later model. The rule is: declare
//      the disconnect with `OrphanAnnotation` (a canonical Any in the
//      entity's metadata) carrying a non-empty `doc` that explains why.
//      A disconnected entity WITHOUT an OrphanAnnotation triggers
//      ORPHAN_NOT_FLAGGED (Warning): silence is the noise the annotation
//      replaces. An OrphanAnnotation with an empty `doc` triggers
//      ORPHAN_DOC_EMPTY (Error): a flag without a reason is worse than
//      no flag. A FLAGGED entity that IS in fact wired into a slice
//      triggers ORPHAN_FLAG_OBSOLETE (Info): remove the annotation now
//      that the entity is connected. The annotation is a humans-only
//      control surface -- tools never set or clear it.
//
// 32. DUPLICATE-SHAPE READ MODELS MUST DECLARE WHY. Two read models in
//      the same EventModel with the same source-event set AND the same
//      field signature are visually a clone: the board shows one event
//      fanning out to several identical-looking stickies and the reader
//      has to mentally re-link what each one is for. Sometimes the
//      split is required (the at-most-one-automation-per-RM rule
//      forces a separate RM per automation consumer; the UI feed can't
//      share a row with the automation feed without coupling them);
//      sometimes it's an accidental clone left over from incremental
//      mapping. Either way the author owes the reader a sentence. The
//      rule mirrors Decision #31: declare the duplication with
//      `DuplicateAnnotation` (a canonical Any in the read model's
//      metadata) carrying a non-empty `doc` that names the
//      differentiating consumer or the consolidation deferral. A
//      same-shape RM WITHOUT a DuplicateAnnotation on either side
//      triggers READ_MODEL_SHAPE_DUPLICATE (Warning). An annotation
//      with an empty doc triggers READ_MODEL_SHAPE_DUPLICATE_DOC_EMPTY
//      (Error). An annotation on a read model that no longer
//      duplicates any other (the shape diverged or the twin was
//      dropped) triggers READ_MODEL_SHAPE_DUPLICATE_OBSOLETE (Info).
//
// 33. SHAPES ARE TYPED, NOT INFERRED. A field-bearing entity (Event,
//      Command, ReadModel) declares its shape as a `Schema` packed
//      into `google.protobuf.Any`. Two entities whose `schema` Any
//      has the same `type_url` AND same bytes are nominally the same
//      type by declaration. Schemas can also be top-level Schema
//      entities (set `id`) and referenced by many field-bearing
//      entities -- the studio renders the schema name on the sticky.
//      This makes the "different read models, same shape" question
//      unambiguous: if you mean the same type, point at the same
//      schema; if you mean different types, point at different
//      schemas. Decision #32 (DuplicateAnnotation) is retired:
//      structural inference is replaced by declared identity.
//      `schema` is the only place an entity's shape lives: the Any
//      holds a `Schema` or a message declared by a TypeLibrary in
//      the same namespace. The inline `repeated FieldSpec fields`
//      these entities once carried is reserved.
//
// 34. READ MODELS SPLIT BY SHAPE, NOT BY CONSUMER. Consumers that
//      read the same data -- the captured-payment row read by the
//      ledger recorder, the refund processor, and the treasury UI --
//      share one RM; splitting into clones makes the board lie, and
//      READ_MODEL_DUPLICATE_SHAPE warns when two RMs in a namespace
//      have the same key, fields and source events. Split only when a
//      consumer needs a different shape. Name the consumers with
//      SharedConsumersAnnotation (Any in the RM's metadata, non-empty
//      doc); the board renders one RM sticky with labeled outgoing
//      edges per consumer.
//
// 35. SCREENS ARE COMPOSITION, NOT MOMENTS.
//      Some user surfaces -- a checkout page, a changeset review view -- are
//      not the output of a single bounded context. They COMPOSE
//      contributions (panels, inserts, side rails) authored by
//      multiple contexts onto one URL-addressable surface. The Event
//      Modeling primitive `UI` continues to be the slice-grain
//      moment (one persona, one observation, one trigger); a
//      separate `Screen` entity sits OUTSIDE the slice graph as pure
//      composition metadata. The four-block grammar
//      (UI/Command/Event/ReadModel) is inviolate -- slices and
//      storyboards reference `UI`, never `Screen`. `UI` gains an
//      optional `ScreenSlotRef slot` so a UI can declare which
//      screen slot it fills; the screen's `repeated Slot slots`
//      declares the contract. The Screen entity lives in a
//      non-context application namespace (same posture as
//      `BoundedContext`/`Domain` per Decision #29) so the schema
//      literally cannot place a Screen inside a BoundedContext.
//      Validators planned: `STORYBOARD_REFERENCES_SCREEN` (Error),
//      `SLICE_REFERENCES_SCREEN` (Error), `SCREEN_NAMESPACE_IS_CONTEXT`
//      (Error), `SLOT_UNFILLED` (Warning),
//      `SCREEN_MANY_CONTRIBUTORS` (Info). Allowlist gains
//      `o.from_field.startsWith('slot')` -- the third legitimate
//      cross-namespace knowledge ref, after the event seam and
//      `realizes`.
//      STATUS: implemented as schema, validators, MCP guidance, and
//      studio support.
//
// 36. RELIABILITY ENTITIES ARE OVERLAYS THAT POINT AT CONNECTIONS, NEVER
//      AT VENDOR QUERIES. SLOs, SLAs, and alerts described a `Connection`
//      through the graph (command handling, projection, reaction, display,
//      integration, or cross-storyboard journey) rather than a generic
//      (from, to) pair, so a target can only be attached to an edge the
//      model actually has. `ServiceLevelIndicator`, `ServiceLevelObjective`,
//      `AlertPolicy`, and `AlertNotificationTarget` are overlays (same
//      posture as `Tracker`, Decision #25): retuning a target or rotating
//      an on-call target never rewrites a design slice. `Signal` is a
//      logical pointer (event timestamps or a named span/metric), never a
//      DataSource query, per Decision #28: the vendor binding belongs to
//      export configuration, not the model.
// =============================================================================

// ---------- Identity & references --------------------------------------------

// Universal identity for every top-level entity in the model -- type
// definitions, slices, storyboards, and the model itself. Nested structures
// (scenarios, field specs, observer details) are scoped to their parent and
// do not carry an Id.
//
// Three parts:
//    - namespace: a free-form string chosen by whoever owns the model, used
//      only to keep one model's slugs from colliding with another model's
//      slugs. There is no enforced grammar, no resolution mechanism, no
//      dependency declaration. If you only have one model, the namespace can
//      be whatever you like (or an empty string). It is NOT a package name.
//    - slug: human-readable name, e.g. "order.placed".
//    - version: explicit monotonic breaking-change counter (semver-major-only
//      at this layer). Doc tweaks, scenario additions, and other non-breaking
//      edits do not bump it -- git tracks those at the storage layer. Refs
//      always carry an exact {namespace, slug, version} triple; there is no
//      implicit "latest" in the data.
//
// Storage layer (out of scope for this schema, documented separately):
// entities live one-file-per-version under a directory-per-kind layout,
// backed by git. The exact point at which a version becomes immutable (per
// commit / per merge to main / per explicit publish) is a policy decision
// not encoded in the schema.
// Identity conventions (contract for every conforming client):
//
//    1. `(namespace, slug, version, kind)` uniquely identifies an entity.
//       The `kind` is NOT part of `Id` because it is implied by the field's
//       static type (`EventRef.id`, `CommandRef.id`, etc.) or by the
//       surrounding `Entity.kind` oneof. Two entities of different kinds may
//       share the same `(namespace, slug, version)` tuple -- they are still
//       distinct.
//
//    2. `version` is monotonic per `(namespace, slug, kind)` and starts at 1.
//       It is a breaking-change counter, never a sequence number. Implementations
//       MUST NOT reuse a version that has ever been written, even after delete.
//
//    3. `slug` is lowercase kebab-or-dot-case (e.g. "order.placed",
//       "place-order-slice"). Maximum length is 200 characters. The schema
//       does not encode this limit; tools enforce it at write time. Empty
//       slug is invalid.
//
//    4. `namespace` is a required, non-empty id component chosen by the owner
//       (e.g. "ecommerce", "billing"), validated by the server on write.
//       Two namespaces are independent: the same `(slug, version, kind)` may
//       exist under different namespaces.
//
//    5. The etag returned alongside an entity covers ONLY that entity's
//       serialized bytes. It does NOT cover referenced entities, scenario
//       payloads packed under google.protobuf.Any, or anything reachable
//       transitively. A stable etag on a slice does not imply that its
//       referents (events, commands, read models) have not changed.
//
//    6. Derived values (NOT stored, computed from the entity):
//       - `EntityKind` is derived from `Entity.kind` (the oneof variant).
//         `entity_kind(entity)` is a pure projection over the oneof.
//       - `EntityRef { kind, id }` for any stored entity is built from
//         the oneof variant + the inner `id` field of that variant.
//       - The "stream" of an Event/Command is `swimlane.id.slug` when set,
//         and otherwise undefined; never derive it from the entity slug.
//
//    7. `supersedes` (on every entity) points at the prior version it
//       replaces. The supersession chain is single-parent and must terminate
//       at a root version with `supersedes` unset.

